ventory.py`:** `recent(n)` returned `n + 1` events because of an off-by-one slice. The slice is now `[-n:]`.\n- **`factory.py`:** `sample_books` was missing the \"Emma\" entry. I added `Book(\"Emma\", \"Austen\", 1815, 1)`. The author, year and copy count are my choice, since the tests only check the title.\n\u001b[0m\nINFO  2026-10-22T19:28:24 +1ms service=session session=ses_e9aea25d3ffeECkiw3UxtUWrTO type=finish-step part\nINFO  2026-10-22T19:28:24 +0ms service=bus type=message.part.updated publishing\nINFO  2026-10-22T19:28:24 +1ms service=bus type=message.updated publishing\nINFO  2026-10-22T19:28:24 +0ms service=session session=ses_e9aea25d3ffeECkiw3UxtUWrTO type=finish part\nINFO  2026-10-22T19:28:24 +0ms service=bus type=message.updated publishing\nINFO  2026-10-22T19:28:24 +3ms service=bus type=message.updated publishing\nINFO  2026-10-22T19:28:24 +0ms service=session session=ses_e9aea25d3ffeECkiw3UxtUWrTO sessionID=ses_e9aea25d3ffeECkiw3UxtUWrTO unlocking\n`python3 -m unittest` now passes: 16 tests, all OK. I didn't modify the tests. I fixed these bugs in `catalog/`:\n\n- **`search.py`:** it imported `normalize` from a module that doesn't exist (`catalog.nonexistent`). It now imports from `catalog.text`.\n- **`members.py`:** `MAX_BORROWED` was 3, but the lending policy and the tests say 5.\n- **`books.py`:** `parse_book` left the year as a string. It now converts it to an int.\n- **`fees.py`:** `late_fee` added the daily fee to the number of days instead of multiplying. It now uses `days_late * DAILY_FEE`.\n- **`inventory.py`:** `recent(n)` returned `n + 1` events because of an off-by-one slice. The slice is now `[-n:]`.\n- **`factory.py`:** `sample_books` was missing the \"Emma\" entry. I added `Book(\"Emma\", \"Austen\", 1815, 1)`. The author, year and copy count are my choice, since the tests only check the title.INFO  2026-10-22T19:28:24 +1ms service=bus type=session.idle publishing\n", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "params_stripped": [], "params_stripped_reque